diff --git a/docs/development/checkpoint-implementation.md b/docs/development/checkpoint-implementation.md
index a057d494f..86140c2c6 100644
--- a/docs/development/checkpoint-implementation.md
+++ b/docs/development/checkpoint-implementation.md
@@ -61,6 +61,7 @@ The manual-commit strategy (`manual_commit*.go`) does not modify the active bran
 - **Reclaiming sessions whose agent vanished** - not every agent fires a session-end hook, and any agent can be killed before its hook runs, so a session can be left un-finalized forever. `SessionState.Owner` — the same fingerprint commit linking matches above — is captured at every turn start by `captureSessionOwner`, and `State.OwnerExited()` reports it gone via `proclive.Check`. `finalizeExitedSessions` sweeps those inside `entire doctor` and `__sweep_sessions`, ending them exactly as a clean stop would. **Not `entire status`**: status is a read-only observer — it reads through `StateStore.ListReadOnly` and reports a dead-owner session as `exited` without finalizing it, because asking what is happening must not change what is happening. Status therefore shows such a session until doctor or the sweeper reaches it. **`OwnerExited` deliberately covers IDLE as well as ACTIVE** — an agent that finishes its last turn and then quits leaves IDLE, so gating on ACTIVE alone missed the common case; only already-finalized sessions are excluded, per the shared `State.IsEnded()` predicate. Liveness is Unknown on Windows and for cross-host state, where behaviour degrades to the `StuckActiveThreshold` timeout. Because the sweep runs inside interactive commands, its eager condensing is capped by `sweepCondenseBudget` across the whole sweep: every candidate is always marked ENDED (a single atomic rename — that is what stops `entire status` reporting it as live), while condensing runs only while the budget lasts, so a multi-day backlog drains over successive invocations instead of stalling one. Skipping a condense is the existing fail-open path — PostCommit retries, and `doctor` reports the session as "ended with uncondensed checkpoint data".
 - **Snapshot checkpoints** (`strategy.CreateSnapshotCheckpoint`, hidden `entire checkpoint create`) - for sessions that change no files, which otherwise never get a checkpoint: turn end skips `SaveStep` when nothing changed, so session end finds no steps and `PrepareSessionEndCondensation` marks the session condensed without writing. A session with pending file changes is refused (`ErrPendingFileChanges`); its next commit checkpoints that work. Pending changes come from `resolveFilesTouched`, not `FilesTouched` alone: most agents emit no per-tool file events, so `FilesTouched` stays empty until `SaveStep` at turn end, and an edit earlier in the current turn is visible only in the live transcript (paths outside the worktree, such as an agent's plan files, are dropped). An edit made through a shell command rather than an edit tool is seen by neither until turn end, so a snapshot in that turn is allowed and overlaps the next commit's checkpoint. The snapshot runs `CondenseSession` inside `MutateSessionState` and returns `ErrMutationSkip`: the lock is required because the per-session redaction prefix cache writes its payload and entry in two steps, and an unlocked writer racing a commit's condensation can leave the entry pointing at the other writer's payload, splicing duplicated or dropped lines into every later checkpoint; never saving keeps the session's window untouched, so repeated snapshots each cover the session from the same start and overlap one another (as carry-forward checkpoints already do; `tokens profile` discloses overlap). It passes `failOnRedactionError` (a snapshot exists for its transcript, so a redaction failure is an error rather than the hook paths' drop-and-continue). No skill telemetry is emitted, since unsaved state never marks the events persisted.
 - **Session-end hooks that run under a host deadline** - an agent whose session-end hook fires inside its own shutdown may be killed part-way through. Such agents declare `agent.SessionEndBudgeter` (Codex: 3s cap, process tree killed on expiry). The budget bounds **only** the eager condense in `endSessionNow`, so the cheap step that un-sticks the session is never the one given up on; it is set below the host cap with enough headroom for the shell wrapper and binary load, which happen before Entire's own clock starts. Unbounded is not the same as guaranteed, though: mark-ended runs under `MutateSessionState`, whose flock acquire blocks, so a concurrent condense holding the lock can push it past the cap and get the tree killed — the exited-owner sweep above is the backstop. The bound is best-effort (condensation doesn't poll ctx between stages), and being cut off costs duplication rather than data: an incomplete condense leaves `FullyCondensed` false and PostCommit retries, except in the window between the v1 checkpoint write and the state save, where the checkpoint is already committed and PostCommit mints a second one over the same transcript range.
+- **Turn-end finalize runs under the turn-end hook's deadline.** `finalizeAllTurnCheckpoints` rewrites every checkpoint of the turn, and each rewrite can fetch from the remote. The per-fetch memos catch a dead network but not a slow one, so the store open and the write loop run under a deadline of `processStart` plus the agent's `agent.TurnEndBudgeter` budget (`agent.TurnEndBudgetWithin` the hook timeout the agent declares or its host applies; built-in agents that declare none get `defaultTurnEndHookTimeout` and must be listed in `turnEndBudgetFallbacks` with their reason (`TestTurnEndBudgetLedger`); external agents always take the default). Only the network phase checks the deadline: transcript preparation and redaction are local and never interrupted, though their time counts against the budget. Cut-off checkpoints are reported once, as capture degradation, not also as finalize errors. Checkpoints the deadline or a host cancellation cuts off keep their provisional transcripts for good (turn start clears `TurnCheckpointIDs`), and `CaptureDegradedAt` makes that visible in `entire status`. The late-transcript and degraded-scanner exits run before the network phase, so they keep their IDs for a repeated Stop regardless of the deadline.
 - **Base commit follows HEAD** - if the user does stash/pull/rebase (HEAD changes without a commit), `syncBaseCommitToHead` moves `BaseCommit` to the new HEAD; amends and rebases remap it through `remapSessionForRewrite`. No git data moves with it, because pending work lives only in session state.
 - **Legacy shadow branches** - older CLIs wrote `entire/<commit[:7+]>-<worktreeHash[:6]>` shadow branches at turn end. Nothing reads them, and nothing deletes them automatically. `entire doctor` reports the strict shape (`checkLegacyShadowBranches`) and deletes it under `--force` or a confirmed prompt, which is the recommended remedy; plain `entire clean` deletes the strict shape too, but it also clears the state of every session based on HEAD, which is the only record of pending agent work, so its confirmation lists those sessions and branches; `entire clean --all` (behind its confirmation) and uninstall also delete the bare `entire/<hex>` form, which a human short-SHA branch can look like. Every deletion goes through `DeleteBranchCLI` (`git branch -D`), which refuses a branch checked out in a worktree.
 - **Orphaned session state cleanup** (`isOrphanedSessionState`) - `listAllSessionStates` deletes a state only when it is ended (`State.IsEnded`: ENDED phase or `EndedAt` stamped), never condensed (no `LastCheckpointID`), and has no pending work (`!HasPendingWork()`). Legacy empty-phase records normalize to IDLE when listed, so they are no longer deleted here (they previously were) and instead remain until `StaleSessionThreshold` expiry. An IDLE never-condensed state is either a live session between turns — a read-only turn, or the turn right after a linked commit — or belongs to the exited-owner finalizer when `OwnerExited` is true. The store is listed by every worktree's hooks, so deleting either shape re-initialised live sessions from zero or erased the ownership evidence before `finalizeExitedSessions` could end the session cleanly. IDLE states therefore remain for lifecycle finalization or `StaleSessionThreshold` expiry.

	// sessionStartHookTimeoutSec bounds SessionStart, which Claude Code blocks
	// startup on. Besides a network refresh bounded by the cli package's
	// trailEnablementSessionStartRefreshTimeout, it resolves the repository and
	// reads and writes session state, which a cold start or a large repository
	// can slow well past that refresh.
	sessionStartHookTimeoutSec = 30
	// captureHookTimeoutSec bounds hooks that snapshot the worktree before a
	// prompt or subagent. UserPromptSubmit already runs this work under Claude
	// Code's 30s default; SubagentStart and pre-task drop from its 600s command
	// default to match. It must outlast gitrepo.StatusWalkBudget so a slow walk
	// degrades on its own before Claude Code cancels the hook and drops the
	// snapshot.
	captureHookTimeoutSec = 30
	// checkpointHookTimeoutSec bounds hooks that write checkpoint data. Their
	// path can spend checkpoint/remote.WriteProbeFetchBudget on a remote probe
	// and gitrepo.StatusWalkBudget on a first-checkpoint status before transcript
	// work whose cost grows with transcript size; a hook cancelled mid-write may
	// not finish that checkpoint.
	checkpointHookTimeoutSec = 120
)

type entireSimpleHook struct {
	hookType string
	matcher  string
	command  string
	timeout  int
}

// entireSimpleHooks lists the hooks Entire registers alone in their hook
// type, each under one matcher (empty for all but SubagentStart).
func entireSimpleHooks() []entireSimpleHook {
	return []entireSimpleHook{
		{"SessionStart", "", agent.WrapProductionJSONWarningHookCommand("entire hooks claude-code session-start", agent.WarningFormatMultiLine), sessionStartHookTimeoutSec},
		// No timeout: Claude Code gives SessionEnd a short exit grace by default,
		// and an explicit timeout would make it wait longer on exit.
		{"SessionEnd", "", agent.WrapProductionSilentHookCommand("entire hooks claude-code session-end"), 0},
		{"Stop", "", agent.WrapProductionSilentHookCommand("entire hooks claude-code stop"), checkpointHookTimeoutSec},
		{"StopFailure", "", agent.WrapProductionSilentHookCommand("entire hooks claude-code stop-failure"), checkpointHookTimeoutSec},
		{"SubagentStart", workflowAgentMatcher, agent.WrapProductionSilentHookCommand("entire hooks claude-code subagent-start"), captureHookTimeoutSec},
		{"SubagentStop", "", agent.WrapProductionSilentHookCommand("entire hooks claude-code subagent-stop"), checkpointHookTimeoutSec},
		{"UserPromptSubmit", "", agent.WrapProductionSilentHookCommand("entire hooks claude-code user-prompt-submit"), captureHookTimeoutSec},
	}
}

// entireToolUseHookCommands returns Entire's pre-task and post-task commands.
func entireToolUseHookCommands() (preTask, postTask string) {
	return agent.WrapProductionSilentHookCommand("entire hooks claude-code pre-task"),
		agent.WrapProductionSilentHookCommand("entire hooks claude-code post-task")
}

// entireHookTimeouts maps each Entire hook command to its timeout. SessionEnd
// is left out because it runs without one.
func entireHookTimeouts() map[string]int {
	out := make(map[string]int)
	for _, h := range entireSimpleHooks() {
		if h.timeout != 0 {
			out[h.command] = h.timeout
		}
	}
	preTask, postTask := entireToolUseHookCommands()
	out[preTask] = captureHookTimeoutSec
	out[postTask] = checkpointHookTimeoutSec
	return out
}

// setMissingTimeout gives every Entire entry in matchers its timeout from
// timeouts, leaving any timeout the user already chose, including one Entire
// cannot model and so keeps as written. Reports whether it changed an entry.
func setMissingTimeout(matchers []ClaudeHookMatcher, timeouts map[string]int) bool {
	changed := false
	for i := range matchers {
062675d75 fix(claude-code): time out Entire hooks so a stalled launch can't block Claude
	// Check at least one of our hook commands is present
	return hasEntireHookInToolHandlers(cfg.PreToolUse) ||
		hasEntireHookInToolHandlers(cfg.PostToolUse) ||
		hasEntireHookInSimpleHandlers(cfg.PreInvocation) ||
		hasEntireHookInSimpleHandlers(cfg.PostInvocation) ||
		hasEntireHookInSimpleHandlers(cfg.Stop), nil
}

// stopHookTimeoutSeconds is the explicit timeout installed on the Stop
// handler. Stop runs PrepareTranscript (short bounded wait) plus turn-end file
// detection and SaveStep, which can exceed agy's 30s default timeout on large
// repos, in which case agy kills the hook mid-turn-end with no trace.
const stopHookTimeoutSeconds = 300

// buildEntireHookConfig constructs the HookConfig for the "entire" entry for
// the host this binary runs on.
func buildEntireHookConfig() HookConfig {
	return buildEntireHookConfigForHost(agent.HookHostIsWindows())
}

